WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.552
This self-join example compares admissions with other admissions for the same patient.

00:00:04.552 --> 00:00:07.527
We give the admissions table two aliases.

00:00:07.527 --> 00:00:13.667
The first alias represents an earlier admission, and the second alias represents a later admission.

00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:18.477
The patient IDs must match so both admissions belong to the same patient.

00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:23.467
We also require the first admission ID to be less than the second admission ID.

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:30.022
That gives the two copies different roles and helps avoid comparing the same pair in both directions.

00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:35.767
Then we calculate the number of days between the earlier discharge date and the later admission date.

00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:40.707
The WHERE clause keeps cases where that gap is between one and ninety days.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:46.047
This is a teaching example of how self joins can compare events within the same entity.

00:00:46.047 --> 00:00:52.922
Similar logic can compare repeat purchases by one customer, repeated service calls for one machine,

00:00:52.922 --> 00:00:55.742
or multiple registrations for one student.

00:00:55.742 --> 00:00:58.477
When you run the query, remember the grain.

00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:04.631
One row now represents a pair of admissions for the same patient, not one individual admission.
